                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1509-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_o.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Omnidirectional" title="Omnidirectional" ><img id="1509-1" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_8.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Bidirectional" title="Bidirectional" ><img id="1509-2" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_sc.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Supercardioid" title="Supercardioid" ></span>Samson C03</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Multi-Pattern Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>Samson C03 is a multi-pattern side-address condenser.</p><p>The mic uses a capsule system described as &#8220;dual 19mm capsules,&#8221; which is unusual for a side-address multipattern microphone. Also unusual is the choice of patterns, with Supercardioid rather than Cardioid (in addition to Omni and Figure-8). The pattern is selected via a small switch on the mic body, below the grille.</p><p>The published polar pattern shows only one frequency, presumably 1kHz. In Supercardioid mode, the side rejection is indeed greater than one would expect from a Cardioid mic: closer to 10dB attenuation, rather than 6dB.</p><p>Two other switches below the grille independently enable a pad (-10dB) and a high-pass filter (-12dB/octave @ 100Hz).</p><p>Visually the mic is very similar to the entry-level C01. Internally the mics are very different, the C01 using a single electret capsule, and the C03 using a dual-diaphragm true condenser capsule.</p><p>The C03 has relatively high self-noise of 23dBA.</p><p>It ships with a hard mount and a carrying case (type not specified). The SP01 shockmount is available separately.</p><blockquote><p><cite>Sound On Sound - 2003</cite><br />I tested this mic with vocals and saxophone, and also used it as a drum overhead, and in all applications it delivered good results, with no obvious faults or problems. The lack of strong presence peaks gives the mic a reassuringly neutral feel, and it captured the subtle detail of the sax very well without sounding strident or honky.</p></blockquote><p><p>Permalink: <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Samson/C03">Samson C03</a></p><p>The mic was released in 2003.</p>
